"""
server.py — Orchestrator Agent
───────────────────────────────────
LangGraph agent that IS the live entry point the UI hits for the
classify / L1-guide / L2-diagnosis / L3-remediation buttons. The UI's Vite
dev server proxies a relative path (see ui/vite.config.ts) to this
process's /chat endpoint -- a real network call reaching this real running
process, matching how the claims-solution-integration reference project
triggers its own orchestrator agents. An LLM inside this one process
decides which of the other 9 agents' MCP tools to call, in what order, for
whichever single action the UI asked for.

Approve/Reject, Close-ticket-and-save-to-knowledge, and plain read
("GET /api/orchestrator/{ticket_id}") stay on app/routers/orchestrator.py's
plain REST endpoints, exactly like the claims solution's own decideApproval/
fetchPendingApprovals -- those are deterministic, form-driven actions an
LLM has no business deciding, so they never go through this /chat agent.

Each L1/L2/L3 tool call here is pure compute with no DB side effect, so
this agent also calls a save_*_result tool (orchestrator_state, this
agent's own MCP sub-app) after each stage to persist the result into
fix_runs -- the same table app/routers/orchestrator.py's GET endpoint
reads, so the UI can throw away this agent's chat reply and just re-fetch
to display results (also matching the claims solution's own pattern:
trigger via chat, display via a separate DB-backed read).

Bound tools, across all 10 agents (9 domain agents + this agent's own
state tools):
  1. classify_ticket_record            (Ticket Classification & Triage Agent)
  2. match_kb_card                     (KB Matching Agent)
  3. find_similar_incidents            (Similar Incident Retrieval Agent --
                                         always run after KB Matching, no
                                         matter whether it matched or not)
  4. collect_l1_info                   (L1 Information Collector Agent)
  5. collect_evidence                  (PolicyCenter Evidence Collector Agent)
  6. diagnose                          (L2 Diagnostic Agent -- only once a
                                         human says to continue to L2)
  7. author_remediation                (L3 Remediation Proposal Agent --
                                         only once L2 found needs_data_fix,
                                         and only once a human says to
                                         escalate to L3)
  8. decide_approval / ingest_closed_ticket / run_ingestion (bound for
                                         conversational completeness, but
                                         the UI reaches these via plain
                                         REST, not through this agent)
  9. get_ticket_run / save_l1_result / save_l2_result / save_l3_result
                                        (orchestrator_state -- this agent's
                                         own read/persist tools)

No ticket-domain agent's own functionality changes here -- this only adds
the real conversational entry point, plus the persistence step each stage
was missing.

Port: 7021 (ORCHESTRATOR_AGENT_PORT)
MCP : http://localhost:7900/api/v1/<slug>/mcp for each of the 10 agents

Run:
    py -3 server.py
"""

